### Why does adding partitions not fix a hot partition?

When you key a record, the partitioner computes `hash(key) % numPartitions`. For one specific key, that formula yields exactly one partition regardless of how many partitions exist. Adding partitions might move the key but it stays on one partition.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.common.utils.Utils;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;

public class HotPartitionDemo {
    static int partition(String key, int numPartitions) {
        return Utils.toPositive(Utils.murmur2(
            key.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8))) % numPartitions;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String hotKey = "BIGCORP";
        System.out.println("Key: " + hotKey);
        System.out.println("  6 partitions  -> partition " + partition(hotKey, 6));
        System.out.println("  12 partitions -> partition " + partition(hotKey, 12));
        System.out.println("  24 partitions -> partition " + partition(hotKey, 24));
        System.out.println("  Still ONE partition in each case -- hot spot remains");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Key: BIGCORP
  6 partitions  -> partition 2
  12 partitions -> partition 8
  24 partitions -> partition 20
  Still ONE partition in each case -- hot spot remains
```

### How does key bucketing/salting work?

You pick a bucket count and derive a bucket from a varying field, then append it to the key. The hot customer's traffic is split across multiple partitions.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.*;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer;
import java.util.Properties;

public class KeyBucketingProducer {
    private static final int BUCKETS = 4;

    public static String saltedKey(String customerId, String orderId) {
        int bucket = Math.floorMod(orderId.hashCode(), BUCKETS);
        return customerId + "-" + bucket;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // BigCorp's orders get distributed across 4 buckets
        String[] orders = {"ORD-001", "ORD-002", "ORD-003", "ORD-004", "ORD-005"};

        System.out.println("Key bucketing for BIGCORP (" + BUCKETS + " buckets):");
        for (String orderId : orders) {
            String key = saltedKey("BIGCORP", orderId);
            System.out.println("  Order " + orderId + " -> key: " + key);
        }
        System.out.println("  Traffic spread across up to " + BUCKETS + " partitions");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Key bucketing for BIGCORP (4 buckets):
  Order ORD-001 -> key: BIGCORP-1
  Order ORD-002 -> key: BIGCORP-2
  Order ORD-003 -> key: BIGCORP-3
  Order ORD-004 -> key: BIGCORP-0
  Order ORD-005 -> key: BIGCORP-1
  Traffic spread across up to 4 partitions
```

### How do you implement selective salting?

Only salt keys that are actually hot. Normal customers keep their plain key and ordering.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import java.util.Set;

public class SelectiveSalting {
    private static final Set<String> HOT_CUSTOMERS = Set.of("BIGCORP", "MEGABANK");
    private static final int BUCKETS = 4;

    public static String computeKey(String customerId, String orderId) {
        if (HOT_CUSTOMERS.contains(customerId)) {
            int bucket = Math.floorMod(orderId.hashCode(), BUCKETS);
            return customerId + "-" + bucket; // Salted
        }
        return customerId; // Plain key -- ordering preserved
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hot customer: " + computeKey("BIGCORP", "ORD-001"));
        System.out.println("Hot customer: " + computeKey("BIGCORP", "ORD-002"));
        System.out.println("Normal customer: " + computeKey("SAV2001", "ORD-003"));
        System.out.println("Normal customer: " + computeKey("SAV3001", "ORD-004"));
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Hot customer: BIGCORP-1
Hot customer: BIGCORP-2
Normal customer: SAV2001
Normal customer: SAV3001
```

### What is the consumer-side trade-off?

Before salting, all of a customer's records were in one partition. After salting, the hot customer's records span multiple partitions, so you lose strict ordering for that customer.

| Aspect | Before Salting | After Salting |
|---|---|---|
| Records per partition | Skewed (one overloaded) | Balanced |
| Per-customer ordering | Strict (one partition) | Lost for salted customers |
| Consumer complexity | Simple | Must merge across buckets |
| Normal customers | Unaffected | Unaffected (plain key) |

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class ConsumerMergeTradeoff {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Consumer trade-off for salted keys:");
        System.out.println("  Before: BIGCORP all events in partition 2 (ordered)");
        System.out.println("  After: BIGCORP events in partitions 2, 5, 8, 11");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  To get complete BIGCORP view:");
        System.out.println("  1. Read from all 4 buckets");
        System.out.println("  2. Buffer and sort by producer timestamp or sequence");
        System.out.println("  3. Merge into unified stream");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Key insight: choose salt field so per-ORDER ordering");
        System.out.println("  stays in one bucket (salt by orderId)");
        System.out.println("  Cross-order ordering for BIGCORP is lost but rarely needed");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Consumer trade-off for salted keys:
  Before: BIGCORP all events in partition 2 (ordered)
  After: BIGCORP events in partitions 2, 5, 8, 11

  To get complete BIGCORP view:
  1. Read from all 4 buckets
  2. Buffer and sort by producer timestamp or sequence
  3. Merge into unified stream

  Key insight: choose salt field so per-ORDER ordering
  stays in one bucket (salt by orderId)
  Cross-order ordering for BIGCORP is lost but rarely needed
```

### How do you roll this out without downtime?

Deploy consumers first to handle both old and new key formats, then flip producers.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class RolloutPlan {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Zero-downtime rollout:");
        System.out.println("  1. Deploy consumers that handle both key formats");
        System.out.println("     Plain: SAV2001 (old format)");
        System.out.println("     Salted: BIGCORP-2 (new format)");
        System.out.println("  2. Add BIGCORP to hot-customer config");
        System.out.println("  3. Flip producers to use salted keys for BIGCORP");
        System.out.println("  4. Old plain-keyed BIGCORP records drain naturally");
        System.out.println("  No downtime, no reprocessing needed");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Zero-downtime rollout:
  1. Deploy consumers that handle both key formats
     Plain: SAV2001 (old format)
     Salted: BIGCORP-2 (new format)
  2. Add BIGCORP to hot-customer config
  3. Flip producers to use salted keys for BIGCORP
  4. Old plain-keyed BIGCORP records drain naturally
  No downtime, no reprocessing needed
```

### How do you choose the salt field?

Choose a field that varies within the hot entity so the ordering you need stays in one bucket.

| Salt Field | Ordering Preserved | Load Distribution |
|---|---|---|
| orderId | Per-order events in one bucket | Good spread |
| timestamp % N | Nothing useful | Even spread |
| Random | Nothing | Perfect spread |

### What are the limitations of key bucketing?

Key bucketing is not a general solution for all ordering problems. You cannot retroactively re-key existing data already in the topic; the fix only applies to new traffic. If downstream processing requires strict global ordering for the hot customer, bucketing breaks that guarantee and you must implement merge logic. Adding too many buckets dilutes the ordering further without proportional benefit.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class BucketingLimitations {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Limitations of key bucketing:");
        System.out.println("  1. Existing data is NOT re-keyed (old messages stay)");
        System.out.println("  2. Strict global ordering for salted customer is lost");
        System.out.println("  3. Too many buckets: diminishing returns, more merge cost");
        System.out.println("  4. Consumer must know which keys are salted");
        System.out.println("  5. Monitoring: must track per-bucket lag, not per-customer");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Rule of thumb: 4-8 buckets for most hot keys.");
        System.out.println("More than 16 buckets rarely adds benefit.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Limitations of key bucketing:
  1. Existing data is NOT re-keyed (old messages stay)
  2. Strict global ordering for salted customer is lost
  3. Too many buckets: diminishing returns, more merge cost
  4. Consumer must know which keys are salted
  5. Monitoring: must track per-bucket lag, not per-customer

Rule of thumb: 4-8 buckets for most hot keys.
More than 16 buckets rarely adds benefit.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Keep the hot-customer list in configuration so adding a newly hot customer is a config change, not a code change. You cannot retroactively re-key existing data; the fix applies to new traffic only.
